<reference xml:id="class.ds-heap" role="class" xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

 <title>The Heap class</title>
 <titleabbrev>Ds\Heap</titleabbrev>

 <partintro>

  <section xml:id="ds-heap.intro">
   &reftitle.intro;
   <simpara>
    A Heap is a tree-based data structure in which the value at the top of the
    heap is determined by a configurable comparator. By default, when no comparator
    is provided, the heap behaves as a max-heap using standard comparison.
   </simpara>
   <simpara>
    Replaces <literal>Ds\PriorityQueue</literal> from v1, providing more
    flexibility through an optional callable comparator that can be passed to
    the constructor.
   </simpara>
   <note>
    <simpara>
     Iterating over a <classname>Ds\Heap</classname> yields the values in the
     order in which they would be popped. The iterator operates on an internal
     copy, so the heap itself is not modified by
     <literal>foreach</literal>.
    </simpara>
   </note>

  </section>

  <section xml:id="ds-heap.strengths">
   <title>Strengths</title>
   <simplelist>
    <member>
     <methodname>Ds\Heap::push</methodname> and
     <methodname>Ds\Heap::pop</methodname> are O(log n).
    </member>
    <member>
     <methodname>Ds\Heap::peek</methodname> is O(1).
    </member>
    <member>
     <methodname>Ds\Heap::copy</methodname> is O(1) due to copy-on-write.
    </member>
   </simplelist>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="ds-heap.weaknesses">
   <title>Weaknesses</title>
   <simplelist>
    <member>Random access is not supported.</member>
    <member>Values are only ordered relative to the top of the heap.</member>
   </simplelist>
  </section>

